1
00:00:00,302 --> 00:00:02,960
Train one AI on nothing but text.

2
00:00:03,214 --> 00:00:05,600
Train a second one on nothing but images.

3
00:00:05,840 --> 00:00:08,612
Never let them share a single training example.

4
00:00:09,006 --> 00:00:12,160
Do they end up with the same picture of the world inside?

5
00:00:12,558 --> 00:00:14,160
It sounds like philosophy.

6
00:00:14,350 --> 00:00:16,356
It turns out to be measurable.

7
00:00:16,654 --> 00:00:25,280
In 2024, researchers at MIT measured exactly this across a wide range of models, and the pattern was strange.

8
00:00:25,806 --> 00:00:30,884
The bigger the model, the more its internal map resembled every other big model's map.

9
00:00:31,280 --> 00:00:33,600
Even a text model against an image model.

10
00:00:34,158 --> 00:00:36,320
They named it after Plato's cave.

11
00:00:36,686 --> 00:00:44,720
Each model only ever sees shadows, pixels, or words, yet the good ones seem to reconstruct the same world behind them.

12
00:00:45,198 --> 00:00:48,612
The formal name is the Platonic representation hypothesis.

13
00:00:49,166 --> 00:00:51,332
Two things were unsatisfying about it.

14
00:00:51,726 --> 00:00:53,476
It was just a pattern in measurements.

15
00:00:53,742 --> 00:00:55,840
Nobody could say it has to happen.

16
00:00:56,270 --> 00:00:59,140
And the pattern suggested the only road there was scale.

17
00:00:59,534 --> 00:01:02,080
Train ever bigger models and the maps come together.

18
00:01:02,382 --> 00:01:07,520
Last week, Jan Lacun and Randall Balastrero showed it does have to happen.

19
00:01:07,918 --> 00:01:10,400
Lacoon is one of the founders of modern deep learning.

20
00:01:10,640 --> 00:01:20,560
Turing award over a decade as Meta's chief AI scientist, and for years the loudest sceptic of the idea that scaling language models gets you real understanding.

21
00:01:21,102 --> 00:01:25,120
Balastrero is a professor at Brown working on exactly these questions.

22
00:01:25,550 --> 00:01:27,360
What they published is a proof.

23
00:01:27,726 --> 00:01:36,240
Under conditions they spell out, two models trained completely separately, different data, different senses, must end up with the same map.

24
00:01:36,622 --> 00:01:41,600
And the proof comes with a twist because it tells you what actually drives the convergence.

25
00:01:41,920 --> 00:01:44,320
Size turns out to be the slow way there.

26
00:01:44,590 --> 00:01:47,600
The lever is what you ask the model to predict.

27
00:01:47,950 --> 00:01:50,160
Their demonstration number is jarring.

28
00:01:50,400 --> 00:02:02,320
A 109 million parameter model trained their way matches a vision model's map better than a 72 billion parameter language model does, after 40 times fewer training samples.

29
00:02:02,702 --> 00:02:04,548
The training approach is called JEPA.

30
00:02:04,846 --> 00:02:08,932
What it changes, why it might matter, and where it breaks, that's this video.

31
00:02:09,294 --> 00:02:11,396
Start with what Jepa changes.

32
00:02:11,918 --> 00:02:18,080
Every AI model is trained on a prediction task, and the choice of task decides what the model learns.

33
00:02:18,446 --> 00:02:25,680
A language model's task is next token prediction, guess the next word, billions of times, until you're clawed.

34
00:02:26,094 --> 00:02:30,596
A video generation model's task is pixel prediction, guess the next frame.

35
00:02:30,958 --> 00:02:33,604
Similar machinery, very different homework.

36
00:02:34,080 --> 00:02:36,260
Take a cup sliding across a table.

37
00:02:36,560 --> 00:02:41,412
If your job is to catch it, you need its position, its speed, when it reaches the edge.

38
00:02:41,710 --> 00:02:45,348
You do not need the exact reflection sliding across its surface.

39
00:02:45,680 --> 00:02:53,636
But a model trained to reproduce pixels has to care about all of it, every glint of light, because the loss punishes any pixel it gets wrong.

40
00:02:54,080 --> 00:02:58,532
A huge share of its capacity goes to detail that no task will ever ask about.

41
00:02:59,120 --> 00:03:02,532
Jeppa's move is to change where the error is measured.

42
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:04,516
Don't predict the pixels.

43
00:03:04,800 --> 00:03:10,400
First, encode the observation into a feature vector, a list of numbers that captures what's going on.

44
00:03:10,670 --> 00:03:14,308
Then train the model to predict the features of what it can't see.

45
00:03:14,640 --> 00:03:21,120
If those features only track things like position and motion, the reflections never enter the training signal at all.

46
00:03:21,454 --> 00:03:23,044
The model is free to ignore them.

47
00:03:23,440 --> 00:03:26,276
Unpredictable detail stops being homework.

48
00:03:26,560 --> 00:03:28,640
This isn't only a thought experiment.

49
00:03:28,974 --> 00:03:32,160
The original VJ paper ran the controlled version.

50
00:03:32,400 --> 00:03:36,160
Same data, same model size, same number of training steps.

51
00:03:36,430 --> 00:03:39,840
One model predicting pixels, one predicting features.

52
00:03:40,206 --> 00:03:48,560
Evaluated frozen, the feature model wins the video benchmarks, 73.7 against 68.6 on Kinetics 400.

53
00:03:48,846 --> 00:03:52,320
Fully fine-tune both, and the gap mostly washes out.

54
00:03:52,590 --> 00:03:57,380
Author-reported, task-dependent, and exactly the shape the rationale predicts.

55
00:03:57,920 --> 00:04:00,228
The training loop has three pieces.

56
00:04:00,560 --> 00:04:07,364
A context encoder looks at the visible part, say a video with random chunks masked out, and produces features.

57
00:04:07,760 --> 00:04:11,428
A predictor takes those and guesses the features of the hidden part.

58
00:04:11,760 --> 00:04:19,876
A target encoder makes the answer key, it encodes the full observation, and its features at the hidden positions are what the predictor must match.

59
00:04:20,240 --> 00:04:24,964
The loss is a distance between feature vectors, no pixels anywhere in it.

60
00:04:25,294 --> 00:04:27,520
Two details keep the loop honest.

61
00:04:27,854 --> 00:04:31,200
First, the target encoder is never trained directly.

62
00:04:31,374 --> 00:04:40,720
It's a slowly trailing copy of the context encoder's weights, and gradients never flow through it, so the answer key can't be bent to make the guesses look right.

63
00:04:41,454 --> 00:04:43,104
Second, collapse.

64
00:04:43,438 --> 00:04:49,264
If both encoders output the same constant vector no matter what they see, the prediction error is zero.

65
00:04:49,582 --> 00:04:53,184
The model aces the exam by making every answer identical.

66
00:04:53,582 --> 00:04:57,984
So the objective adds an anti-collapse term that forces features to spread out.

67
00:04:58,414 --> 00:05:01,424
Pull matching pairs together, keep everything spread apart.

68
00:05:01,838 --> 00:05:03,172
Both halves carry weight.

69
00:05:03,438 --> 00:05:07,664
One confusion worth killing because it's all over the comments under this paper.

70
00:05:07,984 --> 00:05:10,784
Jeppa is not a rival to transformers.

71
00:05:11,024 --> 00:05:15,424
A transformer is network machinery, attention layers, feed forward blocks.

72
00:05:15,730 --> 00:05:17,584
Jepa is a training objective.

73
00:05:17,806 --> 00:05:19,104
What the loss measures.

74
00:05:19,502 --> 00:05:23,904
The 109 million parameter model in the paper is a BERT-style transformer.

75
00:05:24,206 --> 00:05:27,184
The vision encoders it aligns with are vision transformers.

76
00:05:27,502 --> 00:05:30,064
The dividing line is where the loss is measured.

77
00:05:30,318 --> 00:05:33,988
Tokens and pixels on one side, learned features on the other.

78
00:05:34,350 --> 00:05:37,184
So what did the small model actually win at?

79
00:05:37,550 --> 00:05:40,420
The benchmark comes from the original MIT study.

80
00:05:40,704 --> 00:05:44,384
1,024 image caption pairs from Wikipedia.

81
00:05:44,686 --> 00:05:50,304
Feed each image to Dino V2, a standard vision encoder, and each caption to your text model.

82
00:05:50,670 --> 00:05:53,504
Every model arranges its inputs on its own map.

83
00:05:53,774 --> 00:05:55,424
Then compare neighborhoods.

84
00:05:55,726 --> 00:06:01,664
Are the captions that sit close together on the text map the same ones whose photos sit close together on the vision map?

85
00:06:02,030 --> 00:06:05,504
Husky in the snow, Husky on a couch, neighbours on both maps.

86
00:06:05,934 --> 00:06:08,144
Husky and toaster, far apart.

87
00:06:08,558 --> 00:06:11,824
The 109M model scores 0.184 on this.

88
00:06:12,206 --> 00:06:18,384
Q2.572B, over 600 times its size, scores 0.154.

89
00:06:18,798 --> 00:06:21,668
The reason the small model wins is the pairing.

90
00:06:21,966 --> 00:06:27,424
Its training pairs were what the paper calls good views, different descriptions of the same scene.

91
00:06:27,790 --> 00:06:31,396
A dog catches a ball, and a retriever jumps for a toy.

92
00:06:31,664 --> 00:06:33,316
Same photo, different words.

93
00:06:33,744 --> 00:06:40,196
Training pulls the pair together, so what survives in the features is the shared scene, and the wording drops away.

94
00:06:40,782 --> 00:06:46,788
The control models got the opposite, two fragments of one sentence, which share wording, not scene.

95
00:06:47,264 --> 00:06:49,860
And the proof's core idea falls out of this.

96
00:06:50,144 --> 00:06:56,772
Under the proof's conditions, the shape of the learned map is fully determined by which pairs you declare to be the same thing.

97
00:06:57,230 --> 00:07:00,516
Pixels or words are just renderings of the scenes underneath.

98
00:07:00,942 --> 00:07:09,344
Declare the same pairings over the same scenes, and you get the same map, up to a rotation, meaning the same shape, possibly spun around in space.

99
00:07:09,584 --> 00:07:14,784
But wait, how can math guarantee anything about two models trained on different data?

100
00:07:15,214 --> 00:07:18,064
The guarantee is a deal, not magic.

101
00:07:18,510 --> 00:07:28,932
If both models are looking at the same underlying scenes, seeing the same mix of them, and pulling together pairs that share a scene, then their maps must coincide, up to a spin.

102
00:07:29,358 --> 00:07:37,104
The reason that's provable, this style of training computes a canonical drawing of the what counts as the same thing graph.

103
00:07:37,390 --> 00:07:42,224
Same scenes, same connections, the same drawing, just in different coordinates.

104
00:07:42,702 --> 00:07:44,384
And the deal cuts both ways.

105
00:07:44,750 --> 00:07:51,184
Train your text model on nothing but cooking blogs and your vision model on landscapes, and they will not converge.

106
00:07:51,438 --> 00:07:53,504
The proof's own conditions say so.

107
00:07:53,838 --> 00:07:56,544
Good data doesn't matter less under this theorem.

108
00:07:56,718 --> 00:07:58,144
It's the entire game.

109
00:07:58,478 --> 00:08:01,860
The map you get is a picture of what you declare to be the same.

110
00:08:02,382 --> 00:08:05,344
The efficiency number is the one practitioners noticed.

111
00:08:05,646 --> 00:08:17,344
Reaching the same alignment took the Goodview model 21.6 million training samples versus 900 million for next token prediction on the same corpus, 42 times fewer.

112
00:08:17,838 --> 00:08:24,260
Mario Zechner's reaction, sample efficiency is the actual frontier, is pointing at exactly this.

113
00:08:24,784 --> 00:08:27,424
The proof has two honest asterisks.

114
00:08:27,598 --> 00:08:29,344
It lives in an idealised world.

115
00:08:29,504 --> 00:08:36,384
The paper itself says no experiment meets all its conditions, and the small model's texts weren't purely text-borne.

116
00:08:36,622 --> 00:08:42,224
An image captioner wrote two of every three descriptions, so visual knowledge did leak into the corpus.

117
00:08:42,702 --> 00:08:48,612
The controls read the same texts, so the comparison holds, but never saw a pixel comes with that asterisk.

118
00:08:49,024 --> 00:09:02,052
Which is what the viral version, 109 million beats 72 billion, Lacoon was right, skips, on its way to also skipping that the contest is map alignment on one benchmark, not chatbot skill, coding, or reasoning.

119
00:09:02,574 --> 00:09:04,452
Lacun's own comment was the careful one.

120
00:09:04,782 --> 00:09:10,500
The paper he wrote merely shows that the similarity structure of the data determines the shape of the representation.

121
00:09:11,022 --> 00:09:13,220
When the co-author says merely, believe him.

122
00:09:13,872 --> 00:09:16,112
Maybe some of you are wondering at this point.

123
00:09:16,430 --> 00:09:21,312
This model never generates a word or a pixel, it just builds maps.

124
00:09:21,710 --> 00:09:22,756
What is that good for?

125
00:09:23,232 --> 00:09:25,060
Start with the simplest answer.

126
00:09:25,392 --> 00:09:27,312
The two maps are the same shape.

127
00:09:27,566 --> 00:09:29,472
One is just the other, spun.

128
00:09:30,192 --> 00:09:34,180
So to turn a photo into words, you don't retrain anything.

129
00:09:34,382 --> 00:09:35,428
You find the spin.

130
00:09:35,952 --> 00:09:44,484
Take the frozen vision models, rotate their features until they land in the text model's map, and let a small language decoder read them out loud.

131
00:09:44,992 --> 00:09:50,916
The only thing in the whole system ever trained on image caption pairs is that one rotation.

132
00:09:51,552 --> 00:09:55,844
A captioning system assembled from parts that were never introduced to each other.

133
00:09:56,206 --> 00:09:59,392
And there's a live demo on Hugging Face if you want to poke at it.

134
00:09:59,886 --> 00:10:00,868
Step up one level.

135
00:10:01,392 --> 00:10:01,956
Watching.

136
00:10:02,382 --> 00:10:13,312
VLJ from late last year watches video and updates its features every frame, and a decoder turns those features into words only when they change enough to be worth a sentence.

137
00:10:13,710 --> 00:10:15,012
Picture a warehouse camera.

138
00:10:15,472 --> 00:10:17,952
Nothing, nothing, nothing.

139
00:10:18,350 --> 00:10:20,068
Forklift entered aisle 3.

140
00:10:20,592 --> 00:10:27,872
On the author's streaming benchmark, that comes to about 2.85 times fewer text decoder calls at similar caption quality.

141
00:10:28,174 --> 00:10:33,952
The video encoder still runs every frame, but the system only pays for language when there's something to say.

142
00:10:34,190 --> 00:10:43,152
And in a controlled comparison, same data, same schedule, its half billion parameter predictor outlearned a billion-parameter token generating baseline.

143
00:10:43,438 --> 00:10:46,112
Continuous perception, occasional speech.

144
00:10:46,446 --> 00:10:50,432
For monitoring, alerting, tracking, that's the shape you actually want.

145
00:10:50,798 --> 00:10:52,352
Top of the ladder, acting.

146
00:10:52,686 --> 00:11:00,032
In VJ Per2's robot experiments, the video encoder is frozen and a small predictor is trained on robot trajectories.

147
00:11:00,366 --> 00:11:06,352
Given the current view, a goal photo, and a candidate action, it predicts the features of what would happen next.

148
00:11:06,734 --> 00:11:14,192
The robot picks whichever action's predicted features land closest to the goal, planning without ever rendering an imagined frame.

149
00:11:14,446 --> 00:11:16,272
And it works in a bounded way.

150
00:11:16,558 --> 00:11:23,952
Cups grasped 65% of the time, boxes 25%, 16 seconds per decision, goal photos handed to it.

151
00:11:24,238 --> 00:11:26,272
A real robot with real limits.

152
00:11:26,512 --> 00:11:29,952
And this is where the price of throwing away detail shows up.

153
00:11:30,318 --> 00:11:39,312
In an October paper from this same program, an encoder trained on robot video learned to keep the predictable background and drop the moving robot arm.

154
00:11:39,630 --> 00:11:42,432
The thing being controlled vanished from the map.

155
00:11:42,798 --> 00:11:44,752
Which makes a perverse kind of sense.

156
00:11:45,070 --> 00:11:48,644
The background is easy to predict, the arm is the messy part.

157
00:11:49,230 --> 00:11:56,832
The fix was to force the features to support action recovery, the task teaching perception what it wasn't allowed to ignore.

158
00:11:57,230 --> 00:12:01,124
But notice what that fix required: knowing what to protect.

159
00:12:01,518 --> 00:12:06,672
It covers the failure you can anticipate and says nothing about the detail nobody thought of.

160
00:12:07,086 --> 00:12:11,952
For a model meant to serve tasks that haven't been specified yet, that's the open problem.

161
00:12:12,366 --> 00:12:15,524
There's no general guarantee the map kept what you'll need.

162
00:12:15,854 --> 00:12:18,020
So, what is Jepa, in the end?

163
00:12:18,272 --> 00:12:28,132
A way of training models, predict the features of what you can't see instead of reconstructing every pixel or token, and let the detail that doesn't matter go.

164
00:12:28,590 --> 00:12:38,052
It runs on ordinary transformers, it works on any kind of input, and as of this month, it comes with a proof that the maps it builds have a real shared structure.

165
00:12:38,446 --> 00:12:41,072
What it isn't, a replacement for transformers.

166
00:12:41,232 --> 00:12:41,924
It uses them.

167
00:12:42,192 --> 00:12:50,272
A finished agent, every demo still needs decoders, planners, and humans handing over goals, or a sign that language models are finished.

168
00:12:50,512 --> 00:12:53,412
Every working system in this video has one inside it.

169
00:12:53,742 --> 00:13:04,388
As for who's using it today, the Jepa line came out of Meta's AI lab, published openly, and Lacoon's new lab, AMI, is now built around this research.

170
00:13:04,910 --> 00:13:10,992
Beyond that, there's no public information that any Frontier lab is using these ideas in a flagship model.

171
00:13:11,406 --> 00:13:13,152
So that's the bet on the table.

172
00:13:13,312 --> 00:13:17,952
A real proof, working prototypes, one hard open problem.

173
00:13:18,702 --> 00:13:20,112
Do you think Lacoon wins it?

174
00:13:20,462 --> 00:13:25,952
Is JEPA the next big breakthrough, or a beautiful idea that stays a research program?

175
00:13:26,638 --> 00:13:27,684
Let me know in the comments.

